the coronavirus pandemic is having a  huge impact on the restaurant business  including fast  food giants like mcdonald's the company  announced this week that global sales  were down nearly 24 percent  last quarter mcdonald's plans to close  about 200 stores here in the u.s  and it just unveiled new initiatives to  tackle the pandemic  and racial injustice including measures  to address hiring bias  only on cbs this morning president and  ceo kris kamchinski  joins us from mcdonald's global  headquarters in chicago mr tim  kamchinski thanks for being with us uh  and thanks for joining us i wanted to  ask you to start off by explaining what  the new initiatives are  what we just announced yesterday to our  entire system it was uh 12 000 people  joined us across 93 countries  uh was refreshing our company values  these are values that started with ray  kroc  65 years ago and it's something i  believe very  uh passionately about as a leader and so  we announced  uh a set of values five values uh one of  which  as you mentioned in the in the opening  uh was around diversity inclusion so  that's what we announced yesterday  so uh part part of the uh these ideas  came  from your own experience on a global  listening tour of  of mcdonald's workers what did you hear  that surprised you  i think what i don't know if it was a  surprise so much is just  a reaffirmation about how important it  is for us to be talking about values  uh whether it was down to the crew  person all the way up to  my senior team uh there's there's a  strong desire for us to be talking about  values because it's a common vocabulary  it's  it's something that across the globe we  have two million people that work in the  mcdonald's system  uh when we talk about values it sets  expectations  and it ensures that all of us are living  up to those so i think just for  me what i heard on the listening tour  was just a reminder about how important  it is for us to continue to reinforce  the values because it's what  makes mcdonald's such a special company  what specifically will you try to change  and how will you ensure that it actually  happens  i think across all the the values for me  what we're trying to do is just make it  more  part of how we're operating on a  day-to-day basis so one of the values is  about  keeping our uh people and our customers  first  and that in the case of the pandemic  right now is all about safety and  and so we're spending a lot of time  right now in our restaurants making sure  we can keep our crews safe making sure  we can keep our customers safe but i  think the thing about these values is  they're enduring they're not something  that's just a moment in time they last  for 65 years  we just use them to animate our actions  uh as a situation arises so right now  we're dealing with a pandemic and we've  got a set of initiatives there  uh that i've been very proud of how our  systems responded you're now requiring  masks in restaurants  yes we are and and when did you make  that decision  that was a decision that we made last  week uh and that was requiring it for  customers we've actually for for quite  some time required our crew to wear  masks  but we thought that it in light of what  we're seeing it's prudent now that we  also ask our customers  to wear masks in the restaurant as well  and how are you planning to enforce that  we've done a whole training with our  crew in restaurants  about expectations and how to work  with customers and explain to customers  why we have  uh the requirement to wear a mask the  the thing i'm pleased to announce uh  or talk about is is one week into this  the vast vast majority of customers are  more than willing  to wear a mask i think the vast majority  of people the vast majority of americans  understand why it's important to be  wearing a mask  in the event that we do have someone  who's unwilling to wear a mask we've  also done training around de-escalation  but ultimately  we're not going to be asking our crew  people to put themselves in harm's way  if someone is unwilling to wear a mask  and comply with  with our rules that might be where we  might bring in law enforcement  all right the nation reported this week  the magazine the nation that uh  mcdonald's employees  uh scores of women who work at  mcdonald's have accused the company of  fostering a culture  of sexual harassment and actually  punishing workers  uh for speaking out what are you doing  to address these allegations  i think anytime you hear about something  like that it's disturbing it certainly  goes against  everything that that we stand for as a  company everything that i want us to be  doing uh  around diversity and inclusion so the  first thing is  when you hear about a situation like  that it's to go in and find out what  went wrong  and we need to make uh fixes quickly in  those situations we need to understand  how do we do better  and for me this goes back to values  we're going to continue to reinforce  set of expectations one of which is  around everybody should be able to come  to mcdonald's and contribute  and it should be nothing based on more  than the job that they do  here and so there's no place for uh the  situation that you described and we will  address it  um you you're facing dozens of legal  complaints do you believe you have a  you have a problem of workplace  harassment  i'm i'm proud about how our our company  and our crew  uh in our franchisees uh how we treat  people you don't get to be  a brand and a business the size of  mcdonald's without  treating people well being a good  employer but  in a system of our size you will have  situations that  like of which you're talking about there  again when those situations crop up it's  important for us to acknowledge them  address them  and do everything we can to learn from  it and so as ceo that's certainly what  i'm committed to make sure it happens  all right chris kimchimski ceo  mcdonald's thank you so much for being  with us
 

